##Introduction##

This directory contains all the out of tree device tree structures, for external
(also called out of tree) modules, along with Makefiles to support the
building. Objective is to build the external device trees, with the same build
tools being used by mainline kernel. All the modules specific device tree source
files, are to be added under this directory. Each such child directory contains
module specific device tree files, typically one dts file, many dtsi files, and
optionally, module specific DT binding files. Each module specific directory also
contains its own Makefile, needed to build it.

The top Devicetree directory contains Makefile, containing required rules to build
all the children directories(containing device tree source for respective modules).

DeviceTree(Makefile)
|
|--->module1_dir(Makefile, contains *.dts, one or more *.dtsi, and *.h header files)
|
|--->module2_dir(Makefile, contains *.dts, one or more *.dtsi, and *.h header files)
...

###Adding a new module#####
The main concept behind adding new module is almost same as in the standard
Linux kernel device tree. For instance, nearly same procedure is to be
followed, as one would following while adding new vendor specific directory,
under following mainline kernel directory:
<kernel_dir>/arch/<ARCH_NAME>/boot/dts.

Steps to be following:

1. Add the new directory for module under top Devicetree directory:
	mkdir <module_dir>

Copy all the dts, and dtsi files under this newly created directory. Optionally,
if the module used its own DT binding header files copy them under the same
module directory.

2. Create Makefile with following contents.
Add the rule, and license header, to build the dtbo file as following:
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
dtb-y += <dts_file_name>.dtbo

3. Add the module specific directory into the Devicetree

Add the following line in Devicetree/Makefile as following:
subdir-y += <module_dir>

This way, newly added module will be registered into the build framework of
external device tree build system.

If the module needs its own DT binding, add the patch of the DT bindings in
file Devicetree/Makefile.common, by adding following line:
TECHPACK_INCLUDE += <path of module specific DT binding>

###Building the device tree bins for out of tree/external modules###

make KDIR=<main_kernel_directory> ARCH=<arch_name(arm64 by default)> dtbs

Prerequisite: The kernel directory pointed by KDIR, should be built to the
extent of generating .config file.
